  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12a-2024-p23.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "With \\(a=\\tan^2\\tfrac{\\pi}{16},\\ \\) \\(b=\\tan^2\\tfrac{3\\pi}{16},\\ \\) \\(c=\\tan^2\\tfrac{5\\pi}{16},\\ \\) \\(d=\\tan^2\\tfrac{7\\pi}{16},\\) the expression is \\(ab+ac+bd+cd\\) \\(=(a+d)(b+c).\\)\n\nSince \\(\\tfrac{7\\pi}{16}=\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\tfrac{\\pi}{16},\\) we have \\(d=\\cot^2\\tfrac{\\pi}{16},\\) so \\(a+d=\\tan^2\\tfrac{\\pi}{16}+\\cot^2\\tfrac{\\pi}{16}\\) \\(=\\tfrac{4}{\\sin^2(\\frac{\\pi}{8})}-2\\) \\(=14+8\\sqrt2.\\) Likewise \\(b+c=\\tfrac{4}{\\sin^2(\\frac{3\\pi}{8})}-2\\) \\(=14-8\\sqrt2.\\) Their product is \\(14^2-(8\\sqrt2)^2=196-128=68.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Let \\(a,b,c,d=\\tan^2\\frac{\\pi}{16},\\) \\(\\tan^2\\frac{3\\pi}{16},\\) \\(\\tan^2\\frac{5\\pi}{16},\\) \\(\\tan^2\\frac{7\\pi}{16};\\) the sum factors as \\((a+d)(b+c)\\)",
        "\\(\\tan\\frac{7\\pi}{16}=\\cot\\frac{\\pi}{16},\\) and \\(\\tan^2x+\\cot^2x=\\dfrac{4}{\\sin^2 2x}-2\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
